---
title: "Getting started"
description: "Install the edge-enabled CLI, configure it, and run a complete local query with no Qdrant server."
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

This page walks through a complete end-to-end local session: build the
edge-enabled CLI, configure it, create a collection, upsert documents, and
search — all in-process, with no Qdrant server running.

## 1. Build the edge-enabled CLI

Edge support is an **opt-in cargo feature**. The default `qql-cli` build ships
with `grpc` and `rest` only; `--edge` refuses to run until you build with the
`edge` feature (FastEmbed and ONNX materially increase compile time and binary
size).

{% terminal title="Build the edge-enabled CLI" %}
git clone https://github.com/srimon12/qql-rs.git
cd qql-rs
cargo build --release -p qql-cli --features edge
{% /terminal %}

The binary is `target/release/qql`. A default build without the flag would
answer `qql --edge exec "SHOW COLLECTIONS"` with:

{% terminal title="Edge disabled" %}
edge support is not installed; reinstall qql-cli with --features edge
{% /terminal %}

## 2. Configure the local backend

`qql config edge` persists an `edge.json` to your QQL config directory
(`~/.qql/edge.json`, written with `0600` permissions). The key settings are
the data directory and the embedding model.

{% terminal title="Configure edge" %}
qql config edge --data-dir ./qdrant_data --model bge-small-en-v1.5
{% /terminal %}

The command confirms where it saved the file and how to use it:

{% terminal title="Config confirmation" %}
Saved edge configuration to /home/you/.qql/edge.json
Use it with: qql --edge exec "SHOW COLLECTIONS"
{% /terminal %}

For a full list of flags and environment variables, see the
[CLI reference](/docs/edge/cli/).

## 3. Run your first local query

Every subcommand accepts the global `--edge` flag, which swaps the backend
from `QDRANT_URL` to the local embedded storage. Start with a health check:

{% terminal title="Doctor" %}
qql --edge doctor
{% /terminal %}

```text
Connected to the local edge backend (healthy)
Hosts: dense=true multi=false image=false cross_rerank=false
  dense_model: BGESmallENV15
```

Now run the real thing. This script creates a hybrid collection (dense +
sparse), upserts two documents, and searches them. No server is involved:

{% qqlExample title="Local end-to-end script" %}
```sql
CREATE COLLECTION docs HYBRID;

UPSERT INTO docs VALUES
  {id: 1, text: 'QQL runs vector search locally'},
  {id: 2, text: 'qdrant-edge keeps the HNSW index in-process'}
USING HYBRID;

QUERY TEXT 'local vector search' FROM docs USING dense LIMIT 5;
```
{% /qqlExample %}

{% terminal title="Execute the script against edge" %}
qql --edge execute first-local.qql
{% /terminal %}

You can also run the interactive REPL entirely offline:

{% terminal title="Local REPL" %}
qql --edge connect
{% /terminal %}

```text
Connected to local edge
qql> SHOW COLLECTIONS;
```

## Model download note

The first statement that needs an embedding (the `UPSERT … USING HYBRID`
above, or any `QUERY TEXT …`) downloads the model from Hugging Face —
**roughly 1 GB for the default dense model** — and caches it locally. The
download happens once per machine and cache directory.

{% aside type="note" title="The first run needs network" %}
The download is the only network access in the local path. After it completes,
embedding inference and all storage operations are fully offline. Use
`--cache-dir` or `QQL_EDGE_CACHE_DIR` to place the cache somewhere
predictable, and `--show-download-progress` to see the progress bars.
{% /aside %}

If you prefer to skip the download entirely, configure the HTTP embedder
instead (storage stays local, embeddings come from any OpenAI-compatible
endpoint):

{% terminal title="HTTP embedder" %}
qql config edge --embedder http --embed-url http://localhost:11434/v1/embeddings
{% /terminal %}

See [models](/docs/edge/models/) for the full model story.

## Try the SDKs instead

The same local backend is available from each host language:

- Rust: `local_executor("./qdrant_data", false)` — [Rust page](/docs/edge/rust/)
- Python: `pyqql_edge.local_executor("./qdrant_data")` — [Python page](/docs/edge/python/)
- Node.js: `localExecutor("./qql-data", { model: "bge-small-en-v1.5" })` — [Node.js page](/docs/edge/node/)

Each package embeds the runtime directly, so no CLI build is needed.

## What happens on repeat runs

Collections persist under the data directory and are re-opened on the next
invocation — including the WAL, segments, and `edge_config.json`. See
[persistence](/docs/edge/persistence/) for the layout and the rules around
`close()` and multi-process access.

For the boundary of what edge can and cannot execute, see
[capabilities](/docs/edge/capabilities/).
